  "articleBody": "It can be pretty baffling when your business shows up in some AI searches but is completely absent from others, or when the information pulled is just plain wrong. This inconsistency usually boils down to how AI systems process information and the quality signals your online presence is sending. It's not always a quick fix, but understanding the underlying reasons can help you get more reliable results.\n\nFirst, remember that different AI systems (Google AI Overviews, Bing, Perplexity, ChatGPT, Siri, Alexa) might use slightly different data sources and ranking algorithms. What one system picks up, another might miss. That’s why consistent, verifiable facts across *all* your online properties are super important. If your business name, services, or location vary between your website, Google Business Profile, or other listings, AI systems get confused.\n\nAnother key factor is content clarity and authority. AI answer engines prioritize direct, verifiable answers to specific questions. If your website has vague answers, combines too many topics on one page, or lacks clear evidence to support claims, AI might struggle to extract and cite your information confidently. They're looking for 'fact density' and semantic relevance.\n\nAlso, crawlability is huge. If your robots.txt file accidentally blocks certain AI crawlers (like OAI-SearchBot for ChatGPT) or if your website is heavily reliant on JavaScript, it can create barriers for AI systems trying to ingest your content. They prefer clean, low-latency HTML.\n\nFinally, traditional SEO fundamentals still matter. Google has stated that foundational SEO is the basis for AI features. So, if your site has poor internal linking, slow loading times, or a bad user experience, it can indirectly affect how AI systems perceive and prioritize your content.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice:\nIf you're dealing with inconsistent information across multiple locations, complex technical setups, or large content libraries, professional help can be invaluable. A specialist can help identify where the inconsistencies lie and build a strategy to present a unified, AI-ready knowledge base.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-23"
